import requests
import pandas as pd
from django.shortcuts import render
from sklearn.cluster import KMeans
from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression
import json


def chart_view(request):
    # Fetch data from external APIs
    api1_data = requests.get('http://api1.com/data').json()
    api2_data = requests.get('http://api2.com/data').json()

    # Process and combine the data into a single dataframe
    df1 = pd.DataFrame(api1_data)
    df2 = pd.DataFrame(api2_data)
    combined_df = pd.concat([df1, df2], axis=1)

    # Perform statistical analysis on the data
    kmeans = KMeans(n_clusters=2).fit(combined_df)
    regression = LinearRegression().fit(combined_df)

    # Generate chart data
    chart_data = {
        'labels': combined_df.index.tolist(),
        'data': combined_df.values.tolist(),
        'clusters': kmeans.labels_.tolist(),
        'regression': regression.predict(combined_df).tolist(),
    }

    return render(request, 'chart.html', {'chart_data': json.dumps(chart_data)})
```

In this example, we fetch data from two external APIs and combine them into a single dataframe using pandas. We then perform statistical analysis using scikit-learn's KMeans clustering and LinearRegression models. Finally, we generate the necessary chart data, including labels, data points, cluster assignments, and regression predictions.

